## What I built

The NEXT ACTION asked to turn the "Õ(√x) end-to-end verifier" claim — which until now was a *per-n op-count fact* (S506) plus a structural argument — into a **measured scaling curve**. Done.

**Instrumentation** (`compressed_layer.py`): added `_acct_vleaf`, a counter that tallies every *verifier large-table evaluation* — a direct `mle_eval` over a committed √x-size cube, which is the **only** verifier operation whose per-call cost grows with x. Everything else the verifier does (sum-check round checks, `eq`/`le`/`band` point evals, the delegated wiring carry-chain `O(nb·log p)`, the batched-discharge recomputes `O(K·polylog)`) is polylog/Õ(√x) by construction, so this count *is* the leading term. It's split into the **per-layer critical path** (scales with K=π(√x)) vs the **one-time** terms (the two S₀ base closes), instrumented at all six leaf-eval sites, respecting the existing pcs/batch_ub gating.

**`--bench-verifier-ops`** runs three configs (all delegate+structured) over n∈{8..18}.

## The result — the falsifiable claim confirmed

| config | per-layer leaf count | whole-chain `vleaf_ops` exponent α | per-step (Δn=2) |
|---|---|---|---|
| (a) no-pcs | `K·(nb+5)−1` | **0.961** (Θ(x)) | ×4 |
| (b) pcs | `K·nb` (Ub openings only) | **0.998** (Θ(x)) | ×4 |
| (c) pcs+batch_trace+batch_ub | **0** | **0.500** (Θ(√x)) | **×2.0 exactly** |

Config (c)'s total is the one-time `2·2^nb` base closes *only* — the entire Θ(x) per-layer term that (a)/(b) carry has collapsed to a one-time Θ(√x) term. The `comm` slope (~0.60, Õ(√x)) corroborates that the non-leaf verifier residual stays sub-linear and never sneaks a Θ(x) term back in.

The exact leaf-eval count identities are asserted in both the bench and **selftest 22** (over q AND BIG_Q — the count is field-independent). Full selftest passes.

## Honest scope
The metric is the *leading term* (large-table evals only). Config (c)'s grand total is Õ(√x) at effective exponent ~0.6 (polylog above √x), which is exactly the milestone's "leading term Θ(√x·polylog)". The α=0.961 for (a) reads slightly below 1.0 because of the `π(√x)/√x` and `nb` log-factors; (b)'s 0.998 and the ×4 per-step confirm the term is genuinely linear.

